{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And if her father hinders her.</b> If he restrained her from the vow — that is, he annulled it. This 'hindering' — I do not know what it is. But when it says ״וְאִם בְּיוֹם שְׁמֹעַ אִישָׁהּ יָנִיא אוֹתָהּ וְהֵפֵר״ (verse 9), you must say that this 'hindering' is annulment. And its plain sense is a term of restraint and removal, as in ״וְלָמָּה תְּנִיאוּן״ (Numbers 32), and as in ״שֶׁמֶן רֹאשׁ אַל יָנִי רֹאשִׁי״ (Psalms 141), and as in ״וִידַעְתֶּם אֶת תְּנוּאָתִי״ (Numbers 14) — that which you turned aside from me." "<b>And YHWH will forgive her.</b> What is the verse speaking of? Of a woman who took a Nazirite vow, and her husband heard and annulled it, but she did not know, and she transgresses her vow and drinks wine and defiles herself to the dead — this is the one who needs forgiveness, even though it is annulled. And if annulled vows need forgiveness, then certainly those that are not annulled do (see Kiddushin 1)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:13:20.146984584Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}